WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.163
Queues are one of the most common ways to connect concurrent parts of a program.

2
00:00:05.163 --> 00:00:06.196
Producers create work.

3
00:00:06.196 --> 00:00:07.229
Consumers receive work.

4
00:00:07.229 --> 00:00:11.359
The queue is the boundary where ownership, pacing, and shutdown rules meet.

5
00:00:11.359 --> 00:00:16.523
A safe queue is not just a container with a lock around push and pop.

6
00:00:16.523 --> 00:00:25.128
It is a coordination object, and its API should define what happens when the queue is empty, full, closing, cancelled, or destroyed while threads are

7
00:00:25.128 --> 00:00:25.473
waiting.

8
00:00:25.473 --> 00:00:29.603
Condition variables are useful for this pattern, but they require careful habits.

9
00:00:29.603 --> 00:00:36.143
Wait operations should use predicates that describe the protected state, such as work being available or shutdown being requested.

10
00:00:36.143 --> 00:00:40.274
Notifications should correspond to state changes made while holding the associated lock.

11
00:00:40.274 --> 00:00:47.159
Shutdown should be represented explicitly so waiting consumers can wake, observe that no more work will arrive, and exit cleanly.

12
00:00:47.159 --> 00:00:54.387
Without a clear shutdown state, programs often rely on timing, sentinel values, or thread interruption assumptions that are difficult to maintain.

13
00:00:54.387 --> 00:00:57.141
Bounded queues are also a defensive design tool.

14
00:00:57.141 --> 00:01:01.616
An unbounded queue can hide overload until memory, latency, or downstream resources fail.

15
00:01:01.616 --> 00:01:10.566
A bounded queue forces the design to choose a backpressure policy: block, fail fast, drop according to a documented rule, or shed work at a higher

16
00:01:10.566 --> 00:01:10.910
layer.

17
00:01:10.910 --> 00:01:17.450
The queue should also define who owns each work item after it is accepted and what happens during cancellation.

18
00:01:17.450 --> 00:01:25.367
Testing should cover empty and full states, many producers, many consumers, repeated start and stop cycles, and clean destruction while no thread is

19
00:01:25.367 --> 00:01:26.400
left waiting indefinitely.
